// The barrio's loose objects (plants, pots, rubbish, lamps, the fountain, signs, the traffic light) as a table code can
// read before the sheets load. They are the Buenos Aires barrio editor's objects (the buenos-aires repo, main bce71d4):
// each frame's sheet, rectangle, foot, scale, sway, pot soil, lamp and fountain data come from its
// editor/recursos/objetos.js, the collider radii from the COLR table of its editor/js/barrio.js, and the rules for
// solids, lamps and fountains from how its editor/js/exportar.js wrote META.props, META.lamparas and META.fuentes.
// Keys are the editor's names, the ones a barrio package gives its props. The editor names them by kind: yy_z_* weeds
// and grass, plm_z_* plants in the ground, plm_t_* plants for pots (those three sway), u_farol_* lamps, z_* the rest.
//
// Scale: the pictures are drawn at 31.25 px a metre (the LPC characters' scale) and shown at OBJECT_SCALE, like the
// people. The old sheet `viejos` is drawn at 1 and the fountain at 0.9 (each object's own `scale`).
//
// An object's fields:
//   sheet, frame   its sheet (a key of OBJECT_SHEETS) and its frame in that sheet's json
//   size           [w, h] of the frame in drawing px (on the screen, times scale)
//   anchor         [x, y] fractions of the frame: the object's foot, where it meets the floor (the editor's ox / w and
//                  oy / h). A solid's entity stands at its collider's centre, so the barrio export lifted a solid's
//                  anchor until the collider, a circle on the floor drawn at SYN of its width, ended on the frame's
//                  bottom row:
//                    solid anchor y = min(anchor[1], (h - 1 - solidRadiusM * SYN * SF / scale) / h)
//                  with h = size[1], SYN = 0.736 (the standard art's camera draws the floor at 0.736 of its width:
//                  META.SYN) and SF = 18 (world px a metre across: META.SF)
//   scale          the scale it is drawn at (the editor's e, else OBJECT_SCALE)
//   sway           true for what sways in the wind (the editor's mece)
//   soilDepth      pots only: drawing px from the anchor up to the soil (the editor's tierra, measured by eye). The
//                  editor sets a plant (an object of its own, a plm_t_*) on the pot, (soilDepth - 2) drawing px x scale
//                  above the pot's anchor
//   solidRadiusM   the radius, in metres, of the circle cars and people collide with; 0 for a decoration that nothing
//                  collides with. COLR gives it per kind of object (the editor's roles: u_farola, pl_malvon...); a
//                  frame several kinds place takes the largest, as the export did
//   bulbs          lamps only: each bulb's [x, y], fractions of the frame (the editor's luz)
//                  A bulb's height depends on the scene's camera (the light must sit where the bulb is drawn), so the
//                  scene works it out: (solid anchor y - bulb y) * scale / camera.heightScaleY world px of height, the
//                  solid anchor as above (the barrio export's META.lamparas z, in metres: divide by SF)
//   lit            lamps only: the frame to show while the lamp is on (the editor's on)
//   spout          the fountain only: [x, y] where its water leaves and the y of the water in its cup, drawing px of
//                  the frame (the editor's chorro). The export gave the water's start dx = (x - anchor x) * scale world
//                  px from the anchor, at (solid anchor y - y) * scale / (CZ * SF) metres up, CZ = 0.752 (a px of
//                  height rises 0.752 px in the standard art's camera: META.CZ)
//
// Not here: the gr_* frames (graffiti, posters and climbers), wall art the exporter bakes into the package's own
// `grafitis` sheet, and the 57 frames the editor gives no name (vending machines, air conditioners, bottles, a public
// bathroom's stains, crates, trees...). That is why six of the folder's sheets are not in OBJECT_SHEETS:
// karsiori_ac_units, karsiori_bar_cafe, karsiori_flower_pack,
// karsiori_vending_machines, lpc_revised_hojas, mem0ry_public_bathroom.

